  "note": "The gradient direction is expressed in an Earth-Centred Inertial frame (GMST rotation).  A random direction aligns with the CMB dipole at |cos| ~ 0.50 (median); p95 ~ 0.95.  The shuffled-clock null destroys the clock-position relationship while preserving geometry, so the sigma-vs-null figure isolates a genuine clock-position signal from a fitting artefact.",
  "timestamp": "2026-10-29T13:04:09.099988",
  "verdict": "no_inertially_fixed_component__sun_synchronous_signal_remains",
  "headline": "FULL-YEAR RESULT (366 days).  The pipeline recovers an INJECTED inertially-fixed signal at full amplitude (0.30 of 0.30 ns, |cos CMB| = 1.00), so it WOULD see a CMB-fixed field if one were present.  It is not: over the full year the GPS gradient is 5 sigma above the null with CMB alignment |cos| = 0.03 (random) and GPS/Galileo do not agree (cosine +0.92).  The CMB alignment seen over any single ~6-week window (|cos| up to 0.97) is a seasonal alias: the real MEO signal is Sun-synchronous -- across 25 windows spanning the year its direction stays a constant 36 +/- 9 deg from the Sun while swinging 51 +/- 28 deg from the CMB axis.  A Sun-synchronous signal is degenerate: solar-radiation-pressure orbit mismodelling produces exactly this, and so would a heliocentric field component that the 26,000 km satellite excursion cannot resolve from an AU-scale gradient.  CONCLUSION: the MEO route neither confirms nor refutes the TEP field; it rules out only a CMB-fixed MEO gradient, and T4 is not closed by this route."
